{% set local_problem %}
<p>Surface drainage solutions have limits. Splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. But in many Athens properties, that's not far enough. Water that stays near your foundation — even a few feet away — can still find its way back, especially during extended rainy periods or when the ground is already saturated.</p>

<p>Limestone County's varied soil conditions affect drainage patterns. Some areas drain well; others don't. Some Athens properties have grading issues that direct water toward the house rather than away from it. In these situations, surface fixes are just temporary patches.</p>

<p>We see the results throughout Athens: wet basements, crawlspace moisture, foundation cracks, and mold issues. Homeowners try various surface solutions without lasting success because the fundamental problem — water staying too close to the foundation — isn't being addressed.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve this permanently. Instead of moving water a few feet and hoping for the best, buried pipes carry it far enough away that it can never threaten your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. The water exits at a predetermined location — typically at the property edge, into a storm drain, or into a dry well.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or solid PVC pipe, depending on your specific conditions. The system is designed with proper slope (typically 1-2% grade) to ensure water flows away continuously. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>Unlike French drains (which collect groundwater already in the soil), underground gutter drains specifically handle roof runoff before it reaches the ground. They work with your gutter system to create complete water management — from the moment rain hits your roof until it's safely away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

